# {title}

The app is a single page, but how much is visible at once depends on the current viewport.

This article covers the responsive arrangement: which panels are visible and when, where they go, and how the same content moves between a side panel and a slide-in modal.

## Why the layout has to flex

The app has up to three things to display at once:

- the quote workbench,
- the list of mapping cards,
- the live JSON preview.

On a desktop monitor all three sit side by side comfortably. On a mobile phone, all three cannot possibly fit the same screen at once.

Instead, the **two side views** — mapping cards and JSON export — get routed to different homes according to one of four layout modes:

| Layout mode | What's visible                   | Side content lives in                         |
| ----------- | -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| `mini`      | workbench only                   | a slide-in **modal**, opened from the toolbar |
| `stacked`   | workbench above a bottom panel   | bottom panel, toggled from the toolbar        |
| `single`    | side panel beside the workbench  | side panel, toggled from the toolbar          |
| `dual`      | workbench between two side panels | both panels at once, no toggle needed         |

Normally, responsive layouts are handled by CSS alone.

However, because some components need to _branch_ on viewport size rather than merely restyle, here **the media-query facts are also mirrored in JavaScript.**

`BreakpointContext` keeps those facts separate from the layout decision:

| Viewport fact          | Media query                                                                            |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `isWide`               | `(min-width: 1200px)`                                                                  |
| `isNarrow`             | `(max-width: 899px)`                                                                   |
| `isTallNarrowPortrait` | `(orientation: portrait) and (min-height: 1000px) and (max-width: 899px)`               |

These booleans describe what the viewport matches; none of them names a complete layout. The derived `layoutMode` combines them:

| `layoutMode` | Derivation                                  |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------------- |
| `dual`       | `isWide`                                    |
| `single`     | neither `isWide` nor `isNarrow` (900–1199px) |
| `stacked`    | `isNarrow && isTallNarrowPortrait`          |
| `mini`       | `isNarrow && !isTallNarrowPortrait`         |

The components branch on `layoutMode`, not on an individual width threshold. For example, `mini` is the one layout with no aside to host the side content, so the modal takes over. `ToolToolbar` uses the same decision to show a modal toggle in `mini`, an aside toggle in `single` and `stacked`, and no toggle in `dual`.

> The three `matchMedia` queries in `BreakpointContext` must stay in step with the layout `@media` blocks in [`+page.svelte`](https://github.com/ebbmango/quote-slicer/blob/main/src/routes/%2Bpage.svelte). The tall-narrow-portrait predicate is also repeated by the `tablet` custom variant in [`layout.css`](https://github.com/ebbmango/quote-slicer/blob/main/src/routes/layout.css) for the mappings grid. `ToolToolbar` does not encode viewport thresholds itself; it consumes `layoutMode`.

The separate `modal-wide` custom variant, from 600px through 899px, only changes the mappings grid inside its available surface. It is a component-level breakpoint, not an input to `layoutMode`.

## The shared rendering surface

Both side panels show the same two possible views — the [[components/mappings-list|mapping cards]] or the [[data-and-tokenization/export|JSON export]]. Rather than duplicate "which view, faded how" across three places (left panel, right panel, modal), there is a single `DataPanel` component that all three render through. It picks the view by a `view` prop and wraps it in a fade-edge mask: a CSS gradient on all four edges that fades content near the panel's edges instead of clipping it abruptly — which doubles as a signal that the panel scrolls, so the native scrollbars can be hidden.

Because exactly one place decides "maps or json, faded how", the three homes for that content can never visually drift apart.

- The **right panel**, when visible (desktop only), always shows the JSON export.
- The **left panel** shows the mapping cards by default, but can be toggled to show the JSON export — except in the `mini` layout, where it renders nothing at all and the **modal** takes over instead. That way the same content never exists in two places at once.

## The data modal

In the `mini` layout, the toggle in the toolbar opens `DataModal` — a panel that slides in **over** the workbench rather than beside it. A few details keep it feeling native rather than jarring:

- Opening the modal pushes a browser history entry (via SvelteKit's router, deliberately, rather than raw `history.*`), so the device's back button closes it — the gesture a user expects for any overlay. A `popstate` listener closes the modal on back-nav without itself popping again, so a back-close can't double-unwind.
- Switching between "maps" and "json" while the modal is already open does **not** replay the slide-in. Calling open again just reassigns the view and returns early; only the content swaps. The two views fly in from opposite edges — maps from the left, json from the right — so the direction itself hints at which view you are looking at. (Svelte reads transition params only at the _start_ of a transition, which is exactly why swapping mid-open never re-animates.)
- If the viewport leaves the `mini` layout while the modal is open — rotating a tablet, resizing a window — an `$effect` force-closes it **instantly**, with a `forceClose` flag driving the exit duration to `0`. The aside the content belongs in has just become available, so the modal steps out of the way without ceremony.

## Side panels and Text tool

In [[overview#the-four-tools|the Text tool]] there is nothing to show in the side panels yet — no tokens, no mappings. So they are collapsed to zero width and slid off-screen. The moment the user advances past Text tool (see [[tools-and-mediums/tool-transitions#the-arrow-launch-text--link|the arrow launch]]), the grid columns, gaps, opacity, and panel positions animate back to their resting state together, over half a second. Which columns exist at all — that is, how many panels there is room for — is decided by `layoutMode`.
